# "NO CENTRAL SERVER, EVER" — MEASURED, 2026-09-04

Everything below was fetched or computed this session. **MEASURED** = numbers with a source. Where I ran my own probes I say so. NOT REACHED items are listed at the end.

---

## THE THREE HEADLINE ANSWERS

**1. Annual mortality of federated instances.** The best directly-measured figure remains Raman et al., IMC 2019: **across a 15-month window, 21.3% of 4,328 Mastodon instances went offline and never came back** (≈17%/yr). The only vendor-grade modern series is Mastodon gGmbH's own crawler: **9,446 servers (1 Jan 2024) → 8,851 (31 Dec 2024), net −6.3% in one year** — net, so gross mortality is higher. What happens to identities on a dead instance: **followers are lost, posts are never moved, and the migration cannot be executed at all unless the dying server is still up and you can still log into it.**

**2. Has any serverless/federated identity system sustained at community-or-larger scale without a central funder?** Across everything measured here: **no.** Every survivor has either a central funder (Tor: 29–54% US government every year FY2019–24; Mastodon: 86% of 2024 revenue from two gifts; Matrix: Element's £3M/yr in-kind), a corporate operator (Bluesky $100M Series B; iroh/n0 with a $19–$199/mo price sheet), or near-zero cash cost with in-kind hardware (Libera.Chat: **FY2025 operating loss ≈ −3,500 SEK, about US$350**; NTP Pool). The systems with neither died: Sovrin, Ceramic, SSB pubs, Briar (decided to shut down in 2025, reversed to security-only life support July 2026).

**3. What does the relay floor cost, and who paid?** It depends entirely on whether the "relay" holds identities. A **stateless relay is nearly free**: a full-network atproto relay is **$34/month** non-archiving, **$152/month** archiving (Bryan Newbold, measured) — **$0.000009–0.000042 per user-year** across Bluesky's 43M users. An **identity-holding node is 3–5 orders of magnitude more expensive per user**: mastodon.social+online cost **€84,000 in technical hosting in 2024** (≈**€0.32/MAU-year**), and matrix.org's homeserver consumes **~50% of the entire Matrix Foundation's £910,821 FY2025 budget** (≈**£1.23/MAU-year**). The floor was paid, historically, by governments (Tor), one-off philanthropy (Mastodon), a single commercial sponsor (Matrix/Element), token pre-sales (Sovrin), or nobody (I2P, SSB).

---

## A. MASTODON / ACTIVITYPUB CHURN

### A.1 Raman et al., "Challenges in the Decentralised Web: The Mastodon Case," IMC 2019
https://arxiv.org/abs/1909.05801 · https://doi.org/10.1145/3355369.3355572 · N = 4,328 instances, 1.75K online May 2018, 15-month window

- **21.3% of instances went offline and never came back** over the 15-month cycle.
- **Top 5% of instances hold 90.6% of all users** and **94.8% of all toots**. "10% of instances host almost half of the users."
- **98% of instances go down at least once**; a quarter are unavailable ≥1 day at a stretch; **21% for over a day, 7% for over a month**.
- **11% of instances are inaccessible more than half the measurement period.** Only 4.5% achieve 99.5% uptime.
- Mean instance downtime **10.95% vs Twitter's 1.25%** at comparable age (2007 Pingdom data). Popularity does not predict availability: correlation of toot count to downtime = **−0.04**.
- **Outages in just 10 instances would remove 62.69% of global toots.** Top 3 ASes hold 62% of toots; losing them loses **70% of toots** with no replication, **11.4%** with federation-based replication.
- AS-wide outages: **6 observed**; the largest (AS9370 Sakura) rendered **3.89M toots** unavailable simultaneously. Five AS outages cut the follower graph's largest connected component **from 92% to 46% of users**. Removing the top 1% of accounts cuts the LCC from **99.95% to 26.38%**.
- Certificate expiry alone caused **6.3% of all observed outages**.

### A.2 The post-Twitter-migration decay curve — MEASURED, from Mastodon's own annual reports
PDFs: https://joinmastodon.org/reports/Mastodon%20Annual%20Report%202023.pdf and `...2024.pdf`

| Date | Mastodon servers | Registered accounts | MAU |
|---|---|---|---|
| Nov/Dec 2022 (peak) | — | — | **2.5M** (Rochko, via The Verge 2022-12-20) |
| 1 Jan 2023 | 9,780 | 6.36M | 1.50M |
| 31 Dec 2023 | 9,800 | — | 1.56M |
| 1 Jan 2024 | 9,446 | 8.67M | 1.51M |
| **31 Dec 2024** | **8,851** | **9.10M** | **938,000** |
| **4 Sep 2026 (FediDB, live)** | **8,791** | **8.73M** | **965,550** |

- **MAU fell 38% during 2024 alone** while registered accounts grew — activity collapsed, registrations did not.
- Peak-to-plateau: **2.5M → ~0.94M MAU, −62%** in two years.
- **Registered accounts fell 9.10M → 8.73M (−370,000) between Dec 2024 and Sep 2026** while MAU was flat. Accounts vanish when instances vanish.
- Mastodon's own two servers had a **37% retention rate** on the 2022 cohort (TechCrunch, 2023-10-02, reporting the FY2022 annual report).
- FediDB network-wide right now (https://api.fedidb.org/v1/stats, 2026-09-04): **42,846 instances, 14,384,860 total users, 1,146,568 MAU** — i.e. **8.0% of all fediverse accounts are monthly-active; 92% are dormant.**

### A.3 Zia/Bin Zia et al., "Flocking to Mastodon," IMC 2023 (arXiv 2302.14294)
The migration cohort itself was already centralized on arrival: **96% of migrating users joined the top 25% of instances**. **11.58% of instances were down at crawl time.** 30.68% of users who moved to a second instance landed on mastodon.social. 21% of the Mastodon accounts were not new.

### A.4 My own measurement of concentration TODAY (fediverse.observer GraphQL, 2026-09-04)
13,674 live core-fediverse nodes (Mastodon/Pleroma/Misskey/Akkoma/Sharkey/Pixelfed/Lemmy/PeerTube/Friendica/GoToSocial/etc.), 998,074 MAU, 14,409,718 accounts:

- **mastodon.social alone = 26.5% of all fediverse MAU and 23.5% of all accounts.**
- Top 3 = 46.6% of MAU · Top 10 = 54.1% · Top 100 = **77.4%**.
- **Top 10% of instances = 97.4% of MAU and 99.0% of accounts.** Top 1% = 80.9% of MAU.
- Compare Raman 2019 ("10% of instances host almost half of users") — **concentration roughly doubled in seven years.**
- **22.7% of live instances have ≤1 account. 69.9% have ≤10. 25.1% have zero monthly-active users.**
- Hosting concentration: **top 3 hosts = 48.7% of instances, 38.7% of MAU** — OVH 17.8%, Cloudflare 15.9% (20.1% of MAU), Hetzner 15.0%. Countries: DE 2,916 · FR 2,765 · US 1,971.
- Uptime (all-time, observer's own metric): median 99.76%, 10th percentile 96.81%, **7.3% of live instances below 95%**.

### A.5 ⚠ AN INSTRUMENT THAT LIES — and why my own mortality numbers are void
I computed annual mortality from fediverse.observer's `date_diedoff` field (it yielded 2.2–14.6%/yr and a median dead-node lifespan of 445 days), then **collided the instrument**. I probed a random sample of 250 nodes the observer marks as died-off:

- **91.2% of "dead" nodes still return a valid `/.well-known/nodeinfo`** (control: 97.6% of live-marked nodes do). Root HTTP 200: 88.8%. Only **3.2%** fail DNS/connect entirely.
- Named large "dead" nodes still serving today: baraag.net (299,883 accounts), tkz.one, pxlmo.com, hexbear.net, lemmynsfw.com, wirtube.de. Only poa.st was genuinely gone.

**Conclusion: `date_diedoff` conflates "stopped answering the crawler / blocked it" with "died." Every mortality figure derived from it is discarded.** Worse, the roster is survivor-biased: probing the observer's own cohorts gave **97.6% survival for nodes first seen in 2019** and 95.8% for 2022 — implausible against Raman's directly-measured 21.3%/15mo, and explained by the observer pruning fully-vanished domains from its list. **There is no public dataset that can answer instance mortality correctly.** The Internet Archive was offline this session, so I could not reconstruct a historical roster and probe it.

### A.6 What actually happens to an identity when an instance dies — MEASURED from the docs and the code
From https://docs.joinmastodon.org/user/moving/, verbatim:
- **"Your posts will not be moved, due to technical limitations."**
- Migration "does not automatically transfer your follows, blocks, mutes, or bookmarks" — those require manually exported/imported CSVs.
- **Step 6 requires logging into the OLD account with the OLD password, on the OLD server.** If it is down, the move is impossible.
- **30-day cooldown** between migrations.

The two structural fixes have been open for years and are still open:
- **mastodon/mastodon#12423 "Support Post Migration"** — opened **2019-11-18**, **632 reactions, 308 comments, STILL OPEN** (~7 years).
- **mastodon/mastodon#23401 "Allow migration of followers to new instance when old instance is unresponsive"** — opened **2023-02-04**, STILL OPEN.

**Lemmy has no documented account migration at all** (join-lemmy.org tells users to "create another account on a different instance").

### A.7 Named deaths, with what was lost

| Instance | Date | Scale | What happened |
|---|---|---|---|
| **mstdn.plus** | froze 2023-01-30 07:45 UTC | "well over 4000 active users" | Admin went silent and never returned. Author of the report **lost ~640 of 700+ followers — 91% of his social graph** — because the move could not complete. Archive export never finished. DMs permanently inaccessible. Same admin ran mstdn.party (~12,000 users). https://lapcatsoftware.com/articles/mastodon.html |
| **mastodon.technology** | read-only 2022-11-01, off 2022-12-01 | one of the largest tech instances | Admin Ash Furrow: *"A member of my family was very recently diagnosed with a terminal illness… I am exhausted… how little joy I've been getting from being an admin. The server has also gotten too large and too complex for me to administer."* https://ashfurrow.com/blog/mastodon-technology-shutdown/ |
| **mastodon.lol** | announced 2023-02-11, closed 2023-05-09 | — | **The domain lapsed and was re-registered by an unrelated party on 2024-08-23** (RDAP, registrar XYZ.com LLC, currently `inactive` with no nameservers). The entire handle namespace of a dead instance is now someone else's property. This is the identity-hijack surface, measured. |
| **mozilla.social** | announced 2024-09-17, closed 2024-12-17 | Mozilla-operated | Corporate operator exited. |

### A.8 Admin economics — the qualitative side, N=16
Zhang et al., "Trouble in Paradise?", CSCW 2024, https://doi.org/10.1145/3687059 (PDF: https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/10201276/1/Zhang_et_al_2024_Trouble_in_paradise.pdf). 16 admins, 7 countries. Manual moderation, CSAM exposure ("I was exposed to the content of child abuse. And it was a hard day."), personal harassment of identifiable moderators, and unresolved legal exposure (*"I want to find those servers so I can block them, but that could be an illegal activity… I'm not an expert in law"*). The paper reports **no cost figures** — that is a gap in the literature.

