## B. MATRIX HOMESERVER ECONOMICS

### B.1 The Foundation's finances, in its own words

| | Figure | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Community donations vs Element subsidy, 2022 | **$6,000/mo vs $400,000/mo** | matrix.org 2022-12-01 |
| 2023 revenue | £82K → **£364K**; expenses **£440K** | matrix.org 2024-01-30 |
| Projected 2024 full-scope cost | **north of £900K** (£550K staffing / £250K servers / £100K admin) | ibid. |
| 2024 actual | revenue **$561K**, cost **$1.2M**, deficit **$356K**; liquidated **$283K of crypto donations** | matrix.org 2025-02-20 |
| FY2025 (Nov 24–Oct 25) | costs **−£910,821**, net loss **−£310,596**, revenue ≈**£600,225** | 2025 Public Annual Report |
| Element's continuing in-kind | **£3M+/yr** in core development; wrote **>95%** of core code | matrix.org 2024-01-30; element.io 2023-11-06 |

Verbatim, 2025-02-20 (https://matrix.org/blog/2025/02/crossroads/): *"unless the Foundation manages to raise $100,000 of funding by the end of March 2025, we will shut down all the remaining bridges"* … *"the end of the road is in sight"* … the $100K *"would extend our runway 1 month."* The bridges (Slack, XMPP, OFTC, Snoonet) **were in fact shut down in 2025**.

Revenue concentration, FY2025 annual report verbatim: *"We churned a Gold member, and the one left (Automattic) now corresponds to **50% of our revenue**."* … *"**We didn't get any grants.**"* … *"the Foundation is still disproportionately dependent on Element's in-kind donations and financial support, **which is unsustainable**."*

### B.2 The homeserver eats the budget
https://matrix.org/blog/2025/06/funding-homeserver-premium/ verbatim:
> "**20% of the Foundation's expenditure goes towards hosting the matrix.org free and public homeserver.** If we add in the cost of the moderation work done by the Trust and Safety team, the total share of the costs attributable to the matrix.org homeserver account for **almost 50% of all expenditure**." … "before we get to the extreme position of **being forced to turn it off** leaving its **370k monthly active users** in the awkward position of finding a new home"

**One homeserver = half the budget of the entire protocol's foundation.** Matrix has since introduced a freemium tier with attachment/upload caps (https://matrix.org/homeserver/pricing/); the price is still unpublished.

### B.3 Centralization despite federation
Foundation's own figures, 31 Oct 2025: **190M known federated users, 1.15M MAU on the open federation, 234K DAU on matrix.org.** With matrix.org at 370K MAU (Jun 2025), **~32% of the entire open federation's monthly-active users sit on one server.** Note matrix.org DAU *fell* from >250K (Jan 2024) to 234K (Oct 2025).

The Foundation says it out loud (https://matrix.org/blog/2024/03/why-matrix-org/): *"**The Matrix.org Foundation doesn't want to become the Gmail.com or Outlook.com of Matrix.**"*

The only peer-reviewed crawl — Jacob, Grashöfer, Hartenstein, arXiv:1910.06295, snapshot 2018-07-25, 2,003 homeservers / 131,463 users:
- Largest server: **76,271 users = 58.0% of all observed users**. **Top 1% of homeservers = 87% of users.** Only **15 servers had >100 users**.
- The finding that matters for the cost argument: *"the single, largest server is involved in 44.5% of all messages, **sends 88.4%**, but receives only 0.6%"* … *"**moving from a centralized network to a more decentralized one will not improve the load distribution, but can actually make it worse**."*

### B.4 Resource cost
- Synapse documented minimum: **1 GB RAM** to join large public rooms. Dendrite: **2–4 cores + 8 GB RAM**, "more if your user count increases," plus headroom for Postgres.
- On a 2×1 GHz / 2 GiB board (Element's own SBC guide), joining `#synapse:matrix.org` took **911.6 seconds**; presence pegs CPU at 100%; the guide recommends blocking rooms over ~1,500 state events.
- Database bloat, measured in the field: `state_groups_state` **51 GB / 274,929,152 rows** against 14.5M events (matrix-org/synapse#7472); one personal homeserver **shrank from 100 GB to under 8 GB** (levans.fr, 2020-06-11) — **~92% waste**. Issue #3364 on unreferenced state groups has been **open since 2018-06-07**.
- matrix.org's ceiling: **51 TB Postgres**, hundreds of workers, bare metal at Mythic Beasts; a **~24-hour outage on 2025-09-02** with a 5.5-hour restore. A 2024 post-mortem states plainly: *"An extra server introduces a recurring cost and requires additional work that **the Foundation cannot afford right now**."*

### B.5 Homeserver churn
No public tracker measures it. The etke.cc Matrix Rooms Search series (https://api.matrixrooms.info/stats) runs 2024-02-10 → 2026-09-04: servers online **8,837 → 20,232** (2.3×), but **indexed rooms flat-to-down (20,573 → 19,980)** and indexable servers' share fell from ~29% to **20.4%**. One month shows **−16.3% (12,810 → 10,726, Sep→Oct 2025)** straddling the matrix.org outage — that is an instrument artifact candidate, not a churn measurement. matrixstats.org and stats.matrix.org are **dead**.

