## C. OTHER SERVERLESS/P2P SYSTEMS — DID THEY SUSTAIN?

**Sustained (2 of 9), and both have a company and a revenue model:**

**Iroh / n0** — the directly relevant case: *a "no server" system that runs servers, and now sells them.* n0's own docs: *"Public relays are operated by n0.computer and hardcoded into Iroh, available free of charge"* with *"no uptime or performance guarantees."* Pricing (https://www.iroh.computer/pricing): **$19/mo Pro** (10K concurrent connections, 100 GB egress), **$199/mo per region Dedicated**, overages **$0.09/GB egress**. Scale: **>200 million endpoints created on n0's public relays in 30 days** (Iroh 1.0, 2026-06-15). Relay outage 2024-11-05: degraded 11:00 GMT, full outage 17:00, resolved 23:01 — **~1M new nodes onboarded during the incident, 100,000 concurrent connections at failure**, detected internally ~12h late. Relay abuse forced **API-key authentication, default-on from June 2026** — the moment the serverless network acquired an account system. **Free public relay operation ends 2026-12-31 for v0.35x.**
  - **Hole-punching success rates: only partially published.** The two URLs specified do not exist (404; I enumerated all 82 blog slugs). What n0 publishes: *"Roughly 90% of network conditions allow direct P2P connections"* (docs, no methodology/N/date) and *"It's normal to see 95% of data transferred in a connection pass directly between devices"* (a *bytes* figure, not a connection-success figure). Their retrospective states the design principle explicitly: *"Our goal was to get holepunching to work reliably, **even at the expense of compromising p2p purity**. If we would need a small server to help, so be it."* **n0's funding structure and investors: NOT REACHED** — undisclosed everywhere.

**Bluesky/atproto** — never claimed serverless, publishes its costs, **$100M Series B** (Bain Capital Crypto, announced 2026-03-19 for an April 2025 round), **43M users**, ~20 billion public records.

**Dead or dying (7 of 9):**

- **Ceramic** — deprecated by its own operator. 2025-04-17 (https://blog.ceramic.network/the-future-of-ceramic-focusing-on-recall/): **js-ceramic and ComposeDB deprecated effective immediately; ComposeDB and the Ceramic Anchor Service to shut down**; *"ComposeDB-dependent apps will break if not migrated."* Blog silent since that date (17 months). **forum.ceramic.network: connection refused.** rust-ceramic (the anointed survivor): 51 stars, last push 2026-01-21. The company is now **Recall**, an AI-agent leaderboard with an ERC-20 on Base.
- **Secure Scuttlebutt** — `ssbc/ssb-server`, the pub server itself: **last commit 2022-06-17, over four years dead.** planetary.social now displays, above its own unchanged marketing copy: *"We're moving to Nostr."* The VC-backed flagship client left for a protocol with explicit relay servers. Manyverse survives on GitLab (last activity 2026-07-01).
- **Briar** — https://briarproject.org/news/2026-maintenance-mode/ (2026-07-09), verbatim: *"**the project didn't have funding**, we were reluctant to look for funding without having a long-term plan, and so we could only work on Briar in our spare time. **Last year, we decided that we wouldn't realistically be able to solve these issues and so we reluctantly decided to shut down the project.**"* Reversed to security-only maintenance. **35-month product silence** (Aug 2023 → Jul 2026). Funders were all grants (OTF, Prototype Fund, NLnet, NGI, Access Now, Internews…); last funding announcement **2022-12-05**. **No user numbers have ever been published.**
- **Willow / Earthstar** — NLnet/NGI Assure, EC grant № 957073. Complete, candidate-status spec. **willow-rs, the reference Rust implementation, is ARCHIVED.** willow-js dormant since 2025-03. n0's `iroh-willow`: dependabot-only since 2025-11-17. Earthstar has been in **v11 beta since 2024-05-21**. **No shipped end-user application on Willow was found.**
- **Veilid** (cDc, DEF CON 31, Aug 2023) — technically alive, socially dead. Still **v0.5.7, pre-1.0 three years in**; **9 of the last 10 commits by one person** (Christien Rioux). VeilidChat still 0.4.9. HN attention: 180 pts at launch → **4 points (2025-11-12), 2 points (2025-06-05)**. Funding: NOT REACHED.
- **OrbitDB** — alive but thin: **38 commits in the trailing 52 weeks**, nothing since 2026-05-14, one active maintainer, funded by an Open Collective plus a wall of crypto addresses.
- **IPFS / Protocol Labs** — **89 roles eliminated, ~21% of workforce, 2023-02-03** (https://pl.xyz/blog/2023-02-03-crypto-winter-update/). Governance handed to independent foundations Nov 2023; **the core IPFS/libp2p team left PL in April 2024** to form Interplanetary Shipyard. **Cloudflare shut down its public IPFS gateway — no IPFS connection after 2024-08-14** — and redirected the traffic to *the other* centralized gateway (ipfs.io/dweb.link). Shipyard's own admission: *"public gateways became entrenched as the default to access IPFS content on the web to the detriment of the principles of IPFS."* Gateway operators also absorb DMCA carpet-bombing. **libp2p circuit-relay economics: no published cost model, operator census, or reimbursement scheme exists anywhere I could reach — a genuine negative finding.**

### The atproto relay cost, precisely (the price tag for a stateless floor)
Bryan Newbold, https://whtwnd.com/bnewbold.net/3lo7a2a4qxg2l (2025-08-27): **$34/month** — 8 vCPU, 16 GB RAM, 160 GB disk, 1 Gbps; **21 GB relay data**, 30 Mbps sustained firehose, 600 events/sec. Archiving version: **$152/mo + $92 setup**, 12 vCPU / 32 GB ECC / 3.84 TB NVMe, **447 GB Postgres + 722 GB CAR**. Full archive ceiling: **~16 TB NVMe**. Firehose volume (Jaz, 2024-09-24): **24 GB/day baseline → 232 GB/day** at the Brazil surge (**~3.16 TB/month**); Jetstream+zstd cuts it to ~18 GB/day, tested on a **$5/month VPS**.

---

## D. WHO PAID FOR THE RELAY FLOOR

### Tor — the longest-lived volunteer relay floor, and the split nobody mentions
**The Tor Project funds software, not relays.** Relay operators pay their own hosting; there is no compensation program.

US-government share of Tor Project Inc. revenue (Form 990 line 1e ÷ line 12, EIN 20-8096820):

| FY | Revenue | Gov't grants | % |
|---|---|---|---|
| Jul18–Jun19 | $4,868,034 | $2,025,910 | **41.6%** |
| Jul19–Jun20 | $4,400,782 | $2,372,145 | **53.9%** |
| Jul20–Jun21 | $7,413,548 | $2,829,250 | **38.2%** |
| Jul21–Jun22 | $5,999,891 | $3,058,724 | **51.0%** |
| Jul22–Jun23 | $8,470,139 | $3,684,992 | **43.5%** |
| Jul23–Jun24 | $7,287,566 | $2,135,763 | **29.3%** |

Pre-2018 this undercounts — $1.3M–$2.8M/yr of "fee for services" program revenue (US-gov subcontracting) sat on a different line. The 2012 annual report prints **"U.S. Government based income 60%"** for 2011. FY2019 was a genuine stress point: expenses exceeded revenue by $582,900, net assets fell $1.68M → $836K.

**The relay floor itself, from Tor Metrics CSVs:**
- 2007: 1,300 relays, 2.06 Gbit/s advertised. 2026 (to Sep 1): **9,853 relays, 1,320.89 Gbit/s.** Relay count grew **5.4×**; bandwidth grew **641×**. The volunteer body count was **flat at 6.3k–7.1k from 2014 to 2023** while traffic tripled. Growth came from bigger relays, not more volunteers.
- **Exit relays fell from 46.4% of the network (2007) to 11.7% (2017)** — the legally-exposed role is the one volunteers refuse.
- My reading of Onionoo today (2026-09-04, 3,215 running exits, 413.4 Gbit/s): **one operator, nothingtohide.nl, carries 21.7% of all Tor exit traffic. The top 12 contacts carry 71.4%.** The "volunteer" exit floor is in practice about twelve foundation-backed non-profits.

### Sovrin — the full arc, to the dollar
Own annual reports, https://sovrin.org/annual-reports/ (US$ thousands):

| | 2018 | 2019 | 2020 | 2021 | 2022 | 2023 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Support & revenue | 2,956 | 1,897 | 453 | 91 | 120 | 94 |
| Expense | 2,569 | **3,931** | 967 | 134 | 106 | 84 |
| Net | +387 | **(2,034)** | (514) | (43) | +14 | +9 |

Seed money was **$4.3M donated by Evernym from token pre-sales**. 2019 legal spend alone: **$1.5M, of which $1.1M on regulatory advice for a public token distribution.** Ledger revenue was never material: **$2,500 in network use fees in 2019; $17,000 in 2020; $76,000 in 2021.** In 2022 a 90% write-fee discount meant *"Sovrin hereby subsidized the industry with more than $300K"* — four times its annual revenue.

**2020-03-13**: all paid staff terminated. Treasurer, verbatim: *"they brought the 2019 average monthly burn rate of **$328,000** down to approximately **$4,000**."* Inherited debt **~$2.3M**.

Stewards: **10 founding (Jul 2017) → ~60 (2018) → 50 nodes flat 2020–21** (12 added in 2020, only 6 spun up a node, 4 removed theirs). By 2023 the Board **lowered node requirements specifically to reduce Stewards' cloud costs**. Shutdown announced **2025-02-08**, cause stated as *"In 2024, no new Transaction Endorsers joined"* plus *"limited Steward involvement in governance."* **Foundation dissolved by the State of Utah 2025-05-21.** MainNet now runs **read-only on a single virtual cloud server operated by Trinsic — one steward, from sixty.**

### Usenet — the historical parallel
Daily feed volume: **4.5 GiB (Dec 1996) → 82 GiB (2000) → 2.27 TiB (2005) → 62.40 TiB (2020) → 274.49 TiB (Feb 2024)** — ~61,000× in 28 years, on a peering model where every full-feed carrier pays for all of it regardless of demand. Exits: **AOL early 2005** ("growing popularity of weblogs"); **NY AG Cuomo agreement 2008** → Time Warner Cable dropped it entirely, Sprint dropped all of `alt.*`, Verizon cut to the Big 8; **Verizon full stop 2009-09-30**; **AT&T 2009-07-15**; **Microsoft 2010-06-01**; **Duke University — Usenet's birthplace — May 2010, "low usage and rising costs"**; **UNC 2011-02-04** after 32 years. *(Comcast's date: NOT REACHED.)* Usenet did not die; it moved entirely to paid commercial providers.

Server census (top1000.org monthly archives, counted directly): **22,245 distinct servers (Jun 2001) → 780 (Jul 2026), −96.5%.** Contributing servers have been **flat at 80–81 since 2021** — a hard floor of committed operators. Eternal-September, a hobbyist text-only server, ranks **#7 among all Usenet feeders worldwide**.

### freenode 2021 — federation with a single point of governance capture
The largest FOSS IRC network was owned by **PDPC Limited, a UK charity that reported £3,060 in net assets for 2009 and was dissolved in 2013**. In 2017 ownership was transferred to Andrew Lee. On **2021-05-19 at least 14 volunteer staff resigned**; Libera.Chat was founded the same day. On **2021-05-26 freenode seized ~700 registered channels** from migrating projects; on **2021-06-14 it reset the network entirely** with no data migrated.

netsplit.de crawler averages (via Wayback + live):

| Date | freenode users | channels |
|---|---|---|
| 2021-04-30 | **77,905** | 42,001 |
| 2021-06-29 | 41,171 | 29,252 |
| 2022-01-28 | 9,931 | 14,148 |
| **2026-09-04** | **5,191** | **7,918** |

**−47% in six weeks; −93.3% in five years.** Libera hit **16,500 concurrent in week one**, peaked at **51,728 concurrent** in 2021, and sits at **31,519 users / 22,488 channels today** — 6.1× freenode, but ~39% off its own peak. The infrastructure was replaceable in one week; **twenty years of channel and nick registrations were not.**

### XMPP — and the loss of its own instrumentation
Google Talk federated **2006-01-17**; Google announced dropping s2s federation **May 2013**; Facebook dropped XMPP **April 2014**. **There is no publicly retrievable longitudinal dataset of XMPP server counts** — data.xmpp.net 403s, list.jabber.at publishes no totals or history, and neither Crossref nor arXiv has a measurement paper. The federation that lost also lost its instrumentation.

### Mastodon — where the money actually goes
| | 2021 | 2022 | 2023 | 2024 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Revenue | €55.6k | €325.9k | €545k | **€2,191k** |
| Total costs | €23.2k | €127.1k | €473k | **€774k** |
| — **Technical hosting** | — | €16.7k | **€73k** | **€84k** |
| — Personnel | €18.4k | €79.7k | €383k | **€585k** |

**Hosting is the cheapest line by an order of magnitude.** €84K runs mastodon.social + mastodon.online (together ~26.5% of all fediverse MAU); €585K runs six people. **86% of 2024 revenue was two philanthropic gifts** ($1.5M for restructuring, $100K for the web UI); the recurring community base (Patreon €248,531) covers hosting but only 42% of payroll. Patrons **fell 22.9% in 2023**.

Individual instances: **Hachyderm took 30,000 users in 30 days** on four donated Dell PowerEdge boxes in a residential basement (peak day **999.80 GiB RX/TX, 2022-11-21**) and has received **$785 lifetime on OpenCollective — $0.026 per user.** Its founder stepped down in April 2023 and died that August.

---

## E. THE COUNTER-EVIDENCE — WHAT SUSTAINED, AND WHAT THEY SHARE

**Email/SMTP** — ~44 years, and measurably a duopoly at the operator layer. Berezin, RIPE Labs (snapshot 18 Jul 2026, OpenINTEL over Tranco top-1M, N ≈ 659,000 MX-publishing domains): **Google Workspace 21.8% + Microsoft 365 16.8% = 38.6%**; next largest, Proofpoint, **1.9%** — then a cliff. **Self-hosting fell from 44.6% (2016) to 22.4% (July 2026) — halved in a decade.** And Benjamin Mako Hill's personal-corpus measurement: **>50% of the mail he replied to came from Google every year since 2010** — despite self-hosting. RFC 9518 (Nottingham, Dec 2023) names the mechanism: *"if a large MTA decides that email from a small one is unwanted, there is significant impact."*

**DNS** — **2,005 anycast instances, 12 operators, 13 identities** (root-servers.org, 2026-09-04); capacity grew ~154× under a frozen namespace with zero new governance seats. Internally skewed: F/ISC 366 instances, B/USC-ISI and G/DISA **6 each**. Resolver share (APNIC, Huston/Damas, ~303M tests Aug–Sep 2019): **Google Public DNS 9.12% first-resolver / 22.36% of full resolver sets; all open resolvers 28.27%.**

**NTP Pool** — 23 years, **~6,136 servers** (Europe 62.5%), grown from 500 in 2006, on donated machines plus donated hub hosting (Equinix, Netactuate). And the sharpest number in the set — Beverly & Rye, arXiv:2602.12321 (submitted 2026-02-12, 9 months, >15,000 servers): **only 19.7% of active servers are fully independent**, and **an adversary can capture the preponderance of pool traffic in 90% of countries with 10 or fewer malicious servers.** The abuse history is the cost story: **Netgear 2003 — 707,147 affected units, 250,000 packets/sec, 150 Mbit/s** at one university; **D-Link 2005–06 — 75–90% of one volunteer's traffic, with a DKK 54,000/yr (~US$9,081) bill**; TP-Link at 0.72 GB/month/device; CSIRO simply closed its server to the public.

**IRC/Libera** — nonprofit förening, 38 members, **FY2025 annual result ≈ −2,000 SEK, operating loss ≈ −3,500 SEK (~US$350)**, because servers and bandwidth are sponsored in kind. Its notable 2025 cost driver was **legal advice on the UK Online Safety Act**.

**BitTorrent mainline DHT** — ~21 years, no operator. **16M–28M concurrent users in 2013** (Wang & Kangasharju, IEEE P2P 2013, ~2.5 years of continuous measurement). **No population estimate has been published since 2014** that I could reach. Ungoverned survival and unmeasurable survival came as a pair.

### The properties the survivors share (derived only from the above)
1. **Operator-layer centralization did not kill any of them.** Email 38.6% duopoly, resolvers 28.27%, NTP 19.7% independent, root 5-of-12 operators holding ~56% of instances. Concentration killed *entry*, not *operation*.
2. **Near-zero mandatory cash at the node.** Libera's whole FY2025 cash loss was ~US$350; NTP grew 500 → 6,100 servers on donated hardware. **Usenet, the only one with real per-server cost, fell 96.5%.**
3. **The client was already installed and could not cheaply switch.** NTP, DNS, and MX lookup are compiled into every OS. Usenet and IRC required an affirmative client choice — and are the two that collapsed (−96.5%; Libera at 13% of IRC's all-time single-network peak of 240,000 on QuakeNet in 2005).
4. **Survivors had a cheap absorb-and-redirect path for hostile externalities.** Every NTP abuse incident was solved by firmware, redirection, vendor zones, or donated servers — never by recourse. The one operator that could not absorb it (CSIRO) exited.
5. **Replication grew without renegotiating the protocol.** The root went 13 machines → 2,005 instances with no protocol change and no new governance seats.
6. **Participation decays even where the protocol thrives.** Self-hosted email 44.6% → 22.4%; Usenet contributing servers 347 (2006) → 80 (flat since 2021); DMARC enforcement stuck at 46.9%. In every series, protocol health and operator-population health diverged.

---

## WHAT I COULD NOT REACH

- **A correct measurement of instance mortality.** fediverse.observer's `date_diedoff` is wrong (**91.2% of "dead" nodes still answer nodeinfo** — my probe, N=250) and its roster is survivor-biased. instances.social requires a token. **The Internet Archive was offline for the whole session** ("Internet Archive services are temporarily offline"), so I could not reconstruct and probe a historical roster. Raman's 21.3%/15-months (2019) remains the only defensible directly-measured figure, and it is seven years old.
- **n0's funding structure, investors, and a real hole-punching success-rate publication.** The two URLs I was pointed at return 404; no such post exists among n0's 82 blog slugs. Only "roughly 90% of network conditions" (undated, no method) and a 95%-of-*bytes* figure exist.
- Tor's blog transparency posts (all 404 in 2026), hence the widely-cited "~85% US government in 2015" — **unverified**. Also: torservers.net / Wau Holland / Nos Oignons dollar figures; Tor sponsor amounts (never disclosed, for any sponsor, any year).
- Any Comcast Usenet-drop date. Libera.Chat revenue figures. Any longitudinal XMPP server-count dataset (does not exist publicly). I2P financials of any kind (never existed). libp2p circuit-relay economics (apparently undocumented anywhere).
- IPFS public-gateway traffic share (no operator publishes it). Planetary Social's exact SSB shutdown date. NLnet grant amounts for Willow. Matrix Foundation premium-account ARR (chart image only).
- Paywalled: Zembruzki et al. ISCC 2023 email-centralization numbers (IEEE, no OA copy exists); MDPI *Applied Sciences* 13(9):5739 (403); ACM DL PDFs for the IPFS SIGCOMM'22 and SPF IMC'23 papers (403).
- BitTorrent DHT population after 2014; tracker-mortality longitudinal studies (none found).
- **OpenAlex exhausted its daily API budget mid-session** ("Insufficient budget… Resets at midnight UTC") and arXiv's API began returning empty responses under our parallel load; later literature search ran on Crossref, HN Algolia, gh, and direct fetches only. WebSearch was never called.
